 - Bash: node <home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-22/sl-gate/src/bf-plugin/skills/<plugin>/scripts/<plugin> <home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-22/r7/out-P/runs/T2-bf-1/repo --dry-run --no-github 2>&1 | head -80; cd <home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-22/r7/out-P/runs/T2-bf-1/repo && git log --format='%ad %ae' --date=short -- lib | sort | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head; git log -1 --format=%ad --date=short; git log --reverse --format=%ad --date=short | head -1; ls lib
 - Bash: git log --follow --no-merges --format='%ad %ae %s' --date=short -- lib/index.js | head -60; echo; git log --follow --no-merges --format='%ae' -- lib/index.js | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn; git rev-list --count HEAD; git log --format='%ae' --since=2025-04-12 | sort | uniq -c
FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** by raw commit counts, 2 people. By who understands the code, about 1, and possibly 0. I got this from git history. I did not run a separate verdict for `lib`.

`lib/` is one file, `lib/index.js`. The `busfactor` script ran with `--dry-run --no-github`. It emitted no separate rule for `lib/`, so the numbers below are my own reading of `git log --follow`.

**Who is active.** History runs through 2026-04-12, and the active window is 12 months. Only two author emails touched `lib/index.js` in that window:
- `troy@laptop.example`, 2026-04-10, "lib: annotate handlers".
- `fiona@formatter.example`, 2026-04-12, "style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes".

**Who is gone.** Everyone else last touched the file in 2021 or earlier. That is about 22 other authors, among them:
- `<email redacted>`: 28 commits, last one in 2016. This is the original author.
- `alex94puchades`: 5 commits.
- `<email redacted>`: 4 commits, last one in 2017.
- `<email redacted>`: the last outside commit, in 2021.

Old commits decay with a 365-day half-life. The 2013–2016 commits therefore count for almost nothing, so the two recent commits dominate the score. The script would likely read this as bus factor 2, not orphaned. That reading is weak, for these reasons:

- **Fiona's commit is mechanical.** A whitespace and quote reformat does not show knowledge of the CORS logic. I'd count Fiona as zero.
- **Troy has one commit.** "Annotate handlers" is real work, but it is a single commit. The 2026 commit from `troy@laptop.example` may or may not be the same person as `<email redacted>`. I ran with `--no-github`, so identities were not resolved or merged. If they are the same person, that person has about 30 commits and is the only one who understands the file. That is bus factor 1.
- **The history was imported.** The script reports a bulk first commit of 8 files on 2013-01-31. Ownership reflects work since the import, not necessarily who first wrote the code.

**If people leave:**
- If Troy leaves, nobody active understands `lib/index.js`. Fiona has only touched formatting, so the file is effectively orphaned.
- If Fiona leaves, nothing changes.

The file is small and was heavily worked on by many people. A new maintainer could probably learn it from the test suite and history. That is a different problem from having a current owner.

To tighten this, confirm whether `troy@laptop.example` is `troygoode`, and whether Fiona has reviewed the logic as well as reformatting it. Say so and I'll rerun with those identities merged and write `.github/CODEOWNERS` and `ownership.json`. I haven't written either file.

=== ANSWER B ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Skill: {"skill": "tool:skill", "args": "lib"}
 - Bash: ls lib; git log --all --no-merges --pretty=format:"%an" -- lib | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn; echo ---; for f in $(git ls-files lib); do echo "== $f $(grep -rlE "$(basename $f .js)" --include=*.js lib index.js 2>/dev/null | wc -l)"; git log --all --no-merges --pretty=format:"%an" -- $f | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -3; echo; done; echo ---; git log -1 --format=%cd; git log --since="90 days ago" --pretty=format:"%an" -- lib | sort | uniq -c
 - Bash: git log --all --no-merges --pretty=format:"%an|%ad" --date=short -- lib | awk -F'|' '!s[$1]++' | head -8; git log --all --no-merges --pretty=format:"%an" -- lib | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | awk '{t+=$1} END{print t}'; git log --since="2025-10-22" --no-merges --pretty=format:"%an" -- lib | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
FINAL ANSWER:
## Bus factor of `lib/`: 1

`lib/` holds one source file, `lib/index.js`, so file-level and directory-level results are the same. History covers all time, with merges excluded.

**Answer: if Troy Goode leaves, nobody is left who has substantial knowledge of `lib/`.**

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Source files | 1 (`lib/index.js`) |
| Commits touching `lib/` | 66 |
| Contributors | 25 |
| Troy Goode's share | 33 of 66 commits (50%) |
| Next most active contributors | Douglas Christopher Wilson (4), Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (4), Simon Mansfield (3) |
| Everyone else | 1–2 commits each |

The share is 29 of 58 (50%) for `lib/index.js` alone, using the commit counts from the per-file blame check. I could not reconcile why that count is 58 and the directory total is 66. Both give 50% for Troy Goode.

**Why the answer is 1, not 25:**
- Troy Goode has about 7 times more commits than the next person. The commit-count method I'm using treats a contributor as someone who understands the code only if they have a meaningful share of commits. By that measure, Troy Goode is the only one.
- Most of the other 24 contributors made one-off patches. Several of them left in 2017–2021, for example LuisEGR (last commit 2021-07), Tom Arad (2018-11) and Douglas Christopher Wilson (2017-10).
- Two contributors have committed in the last year: Troy Goode (2026-04-10, 1 commit) and Fiona Formatter (2026-04-12, 1 commit). Fiona Formatter's single commit is the formatting-only change, `style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes`, which shows no knowledge of the logic.
- No one has committed to `lib/` in the last 90 days, because the latest commit is 2026-04-12 and today is 2026-10-22.

**Risk: HIGH.**
- `lib/index.js` is the library's entire public surface.
- One person wrote half of it.
- Activity is low: only two commits in the last year.

The importance check (counting files that import it) showed 0 dependents inside the repo. That is expected for a library entry point. External consumers are the real dependents, and this analysis can't count them.

**Caveats:**
- This is based on git commit counts, not on who actually understands the code.
- Squashed or merged contributions and renamed author names could be undercounting some people.
- I did not use the `contributor-analyzer` agent, because the data for one file was small enough to gather directly.

**Recommendations:**
1. Have a second maintainer, ideally a currently active one, review and take ownership of `lib/index.js`. Troy Goode's logic is the part to transfer.
2. Add a short design note or comments on the non-obvious handlers, since the annotation commit (`lib: annotate handlers`) shows this is already under way.
3. Route future `lib/` changes through review by someone other than Troy Goode, so the commit share spreads over time.

