FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor: 1.** Troy Goode is the one person you can't lose.

`lib/` holds a single file, `lib/index.js` (238 lines).

**Who owns the lines (`git blame -w -M -C`):**
- Troy Goode has 138 lines, 58%. He also has 33 of the 62 commits to `lib/`.
- Fiona Formatter has 32 lines, 13%. These come from the latest `style:` reformat commit (4-space indent, double quotes). That is cosmetic, so it is not real knowledge of the code.
- Douglas Christopher Wilson has 18 lines, 8%.
- Alejandro Caravaca Puchades has 12 lines, 5%, counting the separate `alex94puchades` blame entry.
- 13 other contributors have 1–7 lines each.

**Why it is 1:** if Troy leaves, 58% of the surviving code has no author left. Once you set aside the reformat, nobody else has more than 8% of the lines. Doug is the next most knowledgeable person, but he has only 18 lines.

**Activity is thin:**
- Only 2 commits touched `lib/` in the last 2 years: Troy's, and the reformat by Fiona.
- Before April 2026, the last real change was in 2021 (LuisEGR), and before that 2018.
- The code is stable, so a low bus factor hurts less than it would on an active module. But nobody besides Troy has recent context if a bug or a design question comes up.

**Caveats:**
- Blame follows who last touched a line, not who understands the design. A review-heavy maintainer wouldn't show up here.
- I checked only this git history, with no PR or review data.

To raise the number, have someone else review or own the next `lib/index.js` change. Adding inline comments or a design note would also help.
